WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.767
AWS networking basics.

2
00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:05.367
A password is a weak first defense.

3
00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:10.033
Let's see how a VPC makes your database unreachable in the first place.

4
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:15.467
A VPC is your own private slice of the AWS network.

5
00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:19.200
You divide it into subnets, and routes decide who can reach what.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.867
There are two kinds of subnet.

7
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:26.933
A public subnet has a route to the internet gateway, so outside traffic can reach it.

8
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.336
A private subnet has no such route.

9
00:00:29.336 --> 00:00:32.400
The internet literally cannot open a connection in.

10
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.133
So the API goes public, and the database goes private.

11
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:38.067
No route means no way in.

12
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.667
Here is the whole picture.

13
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:42.967
Users reach the internet gateway.

14
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:46.267
It hands traffic to the API in the public subnet.

15
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:49.700
The API talks to the database in the private subnet.

16
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.632
For patches, a NAT gateway gives the database outbound-only access.

17
00:00:54.632 --> 00:00:57.467
It can reach out, but nothing reaches in.

18
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.900
Now the best trick: security groups.

19
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.767
They are firewalls on each instance, and they can reference each other.

20
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:09.476
The database allows its port from the API's group, not an IP range.

21
00:01:09.476 --> 00:01:12.767
Instances come and go, and the rule stays correct.

22
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:15.533
Here it is in the command line.

23
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:21.533
We allow ingress on the database group, on the Postgres port, from the API's security group.

24
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:24.935
Notice what's absent: no rule from the open internet.

25
00:01:24.935 --> 00:01:27.100
It can't connect, not just shouldn't.

26
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.767
Here is why this matters.

27
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:35.233
Your Postgres database holds every embedding for your RAG system, and you don't want it scraped.

28
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:40.067
It lives in a private subnet, and only the API's group can open the port.

29
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:43.400
When a pentest says it's unreachable, that's not luck.

30
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.433
It's this diagram, and you should draw it from memory.

31
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:48.655
Let's recap.

32
00:01:48.655 --> 00:01:51.633
A VPC is your private slice of AWS.

33
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:54.767
Private subnets have no route in from the internet.

34
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:58.267
A NAT gateway gives them outbound-only access.

35
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:03.607
And security groups reference each other, so one rule protects the database.

36
00:02:03.607 --> 00:02:04.867
Next up: SageMaker.
